  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through Thursday)\nIssued at 431 AM EDT Fri Aug 7 2026\n\nHigh pressure builds back across the Florida peninsula by Sunday and \npersists through the first half of the week, bringing overall \ncoverage down to a more typical sea-breeze driven convective \npattern. Thus, scattered afternoon and evening showers and \nthunderstorms are expected each day through the extended period. \nUnder prevailing easterly to southeasterly flow, early morning \nshowers are possible over the Atlantic waters and coastal areas each \nday, with the highest convective coverage in the afternoon favoring\nthe interior under lighter winds as the pressure gradient relaxes\nearly next week.\n\nOther than the diurnal sea-breeze pattern, guidance continues to \nhint at a plume of Saharan dust approaching South Florida Monday \nnight into Tuesday. If this dry air mass moves directly overhead, it \ncould suppress overall convective coverage and rain chances through \nmid-week. However, if South Florida remains on the periphery of the \nplume, the intrusion of mid-level dry air could bring potential for \nsevere wind concerns with isolated storms along sea-breeze \nboundaries. Will continue to monitor the evolution of this airmass \nin future guidance.\n\nWith high pressure in place and a slight warming trend expected \nacross South Florida, heat impacts will remain a concern through the \nmiddle of the work week.",
